<%#
  ONE PUBLISHED PROJECT'S PAGE BODY (stage 3, S3-4; extracted stage 4, S4-4). Two callers render
  it: public/projects/show.html.erb (the visitor's page) and projects/preview.html.erb (the
  OWNER's preview of it, behind a banner, at any visibility — spec §7). Extracted rather than
  copied for the reason Maker::ProjectPhotos was: two surfaces claiming to show "what a visitor
  sees" that are two templates are a pair that drifts, and the drift is invisible until a maker
  publishes something the preview never showed them.

  IT READS `pub` AND NOTHING ELSE. No ivars, no session, no `current_user` — which is what lets the
  same markup render under Public::BaseController (ActionController::Base, no session at all) and
  under ProjectsController without a branch. Its `content_for` blocks stayed behind in show.html.erb
  on purpose: the noindex meta and the Atom <link> are the PUBLIC page's head, not the preview's.

  Local: pub (Maker::PublicProject).
%>
<% project = pub.project %>
<%# THE TOP OF THE PAGE (Shop manual slice 8; the `public-project` screen): the head at the left and
    the cover, the project's own figure, at the right, wrapping under it on a narrow window.

    `.page-head` IS A TITLE, AT MOST A LEAD, AND AT MOST ONE ROW OF CONTROLS (template_hygiene_test.rb,
    "every .page-head is a title, at most a lead, and at most one row of controls"): `.page-title`
    holds the caps line, the name and the description, and `.head-actions` the page's one action, the
    bundle, and the feed. This head once passed three slots, and the byline was laid out in the
    centre of the page between the name and the button.

    THE CAPS LINE IS THE PAGE'S ONE BREADCRUMB LANDMARK, `nav.breadcrumb` around the byline: the way up
    is the maker, so "A project by @ada" is the path, and the owner's preview (projects/preview)
    renders this partial inside a shell whose own caps path is the page title, so one landmark is all
    either page carries (project_preview_test.rb). The badges ride in it, as the facts beside the
    name. %>
<div class="public-top">
  <div class="page-head public-head">
    <div class="page-title">
      <nav class="breadcrumb public-path" aria-label="Project path">
        <p class="public-byline">
          <span>A project by <%= link_to "@#{pub.handle}", public_user_path(pub.handle) %></span>
          <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>updated <%= time_at(project.updated_at, style: :date) %></span>
          <%# THE SAME RULE AS THE MAKER'S OWN PAGE (stage 4, S4-6): `planning` is the column default and
              badging a default is noise; a status the maker SET is worth a badge. Found in review: the
              status variants were restyled in components.css and this page went on badging the default. %>
          <% unless project.status == "planning" %>
            <span class="badge status-<%= project.status %>"><%= project.status.humanize %></span>
          <% end %>
          <% if project.license_label %>
            <span class="badge license"><%= project.license_label %></span>
          <% end %>
          <% if project.unlisted? %>
            <span class="badge visibility-unlisted">Unlisted link</span>
          <% end %>
        </p>
      </nav>
      <h1><%= project.name %></h1>
      <% if project.description.present? %>
        <p class="page-lead project-description"><%= project.description %></p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% bundle = pub.bundle_files %>
    <% feed = pub.panel_visible?("build_log") || pub.panel_visible?("releases") %>
    <% if bundle.any? || feed %>
      <div class="head-actions">
        <%# STAGE 3, S3-6: THE BUNDLE, BUILT IN THE VISITOR'S BROWSER. The button hands
            `bundle_controller.js` a manifest URL and nothing else; that controller fetches each file
            through the SAME public route this page links, adds bom.csv, and zips it client-side, so no
            byte passes through Rails that a plain download would not already have moved (§15). The
            status line is where a refused fetch is reported -- on the s3 deployment the public file
            route redirects to a presigned URL at the bucket, a cross-origin fetch that reads only
            under the bucket's CORS rule (docs/guides/DEPLOY.md) and the connect-src the port names for the
            CSP, so the button must SAY it failed rather than sit there having done nothing.

            `bundle.any?` IS THE ONLY CONDITION AND NO PANEL GATE JOINS IT (hardening, 2026-09-08;
            tinkerex#283). The three other side documents are gated on the panel they belong to; this one
            has no panel to be hidden by, because `attachments` is in Maker::ProjectKind::ALWAYS and no
            override may hide the folder. So the empty test covers the one case that exists -- a folder
            with nothing to zip -- and a second condition here would be a branch nothing can take (the
            argument in full at Maker::PublicProject#bundle_files). The BOM inside the zip IS gated: the
            manifest's `bom_csv_url` is null when that panel is hidden and the script skips it.

            THE PAGE'S ONE PRIMARY (Shop manual slice 8): taking the whole project away is what a
            visitor comes for, so it is the ink fill (design-system.md rule 13). %>
        <% if bundle.any? %>
          <div class="page-actions bundle" data-controller="bundle"
               data-bundle-manifest-value="<%= public_bundle_path(pub.handle, project.slug, **pub.link_params) %>"
               data-bundle-name-value="<%= project.slug %>">
            <button type="button" class="btn primary" data-action="bundle#build">
              <%# "FIRST N FILES" WHEN THE LISTING HIT ITS CAP, the same fact the manifest carries as
                  `truncated`. A button that said "300 files" for a folder holding 900 would be a lie the
                  visitor cannot detect until they open the zip. %>
              Download bundle (<%= "first " if pub.listing.truncated? %><%= pluralize(bundle.size, "file") %>, <%= number_to_human_size(bundle.sum(&:size)) %>)
            </button>
            <span class="muted" data-bundle-target="status"></span>
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <%# NO LINK TO A FEED WITH NOTHING IN IT (hardening, 2026-09-08; tinkerex#283). The feed carries
            exactly two kinds, and a maker who hid both panels publishes neither -- so the document is
            still valid Atom with zero entries, and advertising it here would invite a reader to
            subscribe to a stream that can never say anything. EITHER panel is enough: one visible kind
            is a feed worth following. The Atom <link rel="alternate"> in public/projects/show.html.erb
            is gated on the same pair and must stay in step -- a reader finds the feed through that
            <link> as readily as through this anchor. %>
        <% if feed %>
          <%= link_to "Follow the feed", public_feed_path(pub.handle, project.slug, **pub.link_params), class: "feed-link linkbtn" %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

<%# THE HERO (stage 4, S4-5; story 5.7): the same picture the private page has led with since S4-1,
    in the same place on it, on the page a visitor sees. An image-role cover is the picture itself
    through the public BYTE route; any other cover is its READY server thumbnail. NO POLL and NO
    placeholder box — /files/thumbnails is session-authed, so a spinner here would turn forever, and
    a page with no cover simply has no hero, exactly as the private one.

    `pub.cover_node` is the SHOWN cover: a chosen cover is a raw column nothing validates, so one the
    project does not permit, or one under a folder the maker hid, renders nothing rather than
    becoming the page's largest picture. The cover is the FIRST term of `pub.thumbs` wherever it
    lives, in the folder or attached from outside it (2026-10-19, F-S14-1). The map is already
    memoised for the page, so the hero costs no query of its own and shows whatever thumbnail is
    READY. A pending one shows nothing, which is the honest answer on a page that cannot poll.

    Since slice 8 it stands at the head's right, its name under it in the mono of a caption. %>
<% if (cover = pub.cover_node) %>
  <%# THE COVER IS FIG. 1 HERE TOO (the Shop manual spec §9; Maker::PublicProject#figure_numbers): the
      26px disc at the picture's top left. %>
  <% cover_number = pub.figure_number(cover.node_id) %>
  <%= tag.div class: "project-hero", data: (cover_number ? { fig: cover_number } : {}) do %>
    <%= link_to pub.view_url_for(cover.node_id), class: "project-hero-link" do %>
      <%= fig_num_tag(cover_number, size: :hero) %>
      <%# `inline_image?`, NOT the role (tinkerex#285): the same split projects/_hero makes. %>
      <% if Maker::FileAttachment.inline_image?(cover.name) %>
        <%= image_tag pub.file_url_for(cover.node_id), class: "project-hero-img", alt: cover.name %>
      <% else %>
        <% cover_thumb = pub.thumbs.thumb(cover.node_id) %>
        <%# THE PUBLIC PREVIEW ROUTE, NEVER `url_for(cover_thumb.asset)` (hardening, 2026-09-08;
            tinkerex#278): a blob URL is unauthenticated and never expires, so the page's LARGEST
            picture was the one that outlived the publication longest. `pub.preview_url_for` carries
            this project's link_params, so an unlisted project's hero keeps its `k`. %>
        <%# A RENDER SITS ON THE CARD, multiplied, like every thumbnail (design-system.md rule 5); the
            photo branch above does not, being the file's own picture. %>
        <% if cover_thumb&.showable? %>
          <div class="render-card project-hero-render">
            <%= image_tag pub.preview_url_for(cover.node_id), class: "project-hero-img",
                          alt: "Preview of #{cover.name}" %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
    <span class="project-hero-name mono muted"><%= cover.name %></span>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
</div>

<%# NOT `tag_chips(project)`: that helper links into /tags/:name, which is the SIGNED-IN owner's
    private tag page. A visitor's tag link belongs on /explore. %>
<% if project.tags.any? %>
  <div class="tag-chips public-tags">
    <% project.tags.sort_by(&:name).each do |t| %>
      <%= link_to t.name, public_explore_path(tag: t.name), class: "tag-chip" %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%#
  THE PANELS, IN THE OWNER'S OWN ORDER (stage 4, S4-7; story 6.5). The visitor sees the page the
  maker arranged: a panel they hid is hidden here too, and a panel they moved is moved here too --
  one layout, read off the project through Maker::PublicProject#visible_panels, so the maker's page
  and a visitor's cannot disagree about what is hidden.

  AND IT IS THE PUBLISHED VIEW (2026-11-19; the Shop manual's spec §3 and §9): a panel the maker
  left off the public page on the Publishing tab is hidden here and nowhere else, because
  `pub.layout` is `Layout#published`. Nothing on this page asks about it a second time.

  WHICH PANELS HAVE A PUBLIC FACE IS Maker::PublicProject::PANEL_PARTIALS (the map lived here until
  the Publishing tab had to list the same panels): `tasks`, `sheets`, `calculations` and `models`
  are absent from it and are therefore absent from a visitor's page WHATEVER the order says.

  EVERY PARTIAL IS NAMED WITH ITS FULL PATH, AND MUST STAY THAT WAY -- including `repo_card` below,
  and the map's own entries (Maker::PublicProject::PANEL_PARTIALS says why).

  WIRING IS THE ONE SPECIAL CASE and it is not new: the shared projects/_wiring partial is what the
  visitor gets, with `editable: false`, and it has always been wrapped in a check for any
  connections at all -- read-only, its empty state is a heading and nothing else, which is a panel
  rather than the absence the rest of this page uses.

  AN EMPTY PANEL IS STILL OMITTED. Each partial below decides that for itself, exactly as before:
  the layout decides whether a panel is RENDERED, the partial decides whether it has anything to
  render. Two questions, the same pair the private page keeps as hidden versus empty — and the
  folder panel joins the OMITTED side rather than the maker's quiet one, because an empty heading
  invites someone to add the first file, which is exactly what a visitor cannot do.

  A BLOCK KEY IS THE FOURTH BRANCH (2026-09-09, content blocks; spec §6), and it is the one that is
  not in the map below for the opposite reason to `tasks`: a block IS published — it is what the
  maker wrote for this page — but its key names a row rather than a partial, so it resolves through
  `pub.blocks_by_key` exactly as a folder key resolves through `pub.folder_dir`. A HIDDEN block
  never reaches this loop (`visible_panels`), and an image block whose picture is not permitted or
  sits under a hidden folder renders nothing, so its slot is omitted like every other empty panel.

  A FOLDER KEY IS THE THIRD BRANCH (2026-09-08, folder panels; spec §5). Every DEPTH-ONE subfolder
  of the project folder is a panel of its own, in the maker's own order, with the view the maker
  chose — so `attachments` above is the folder ROOT alone (spec decision 4) and a hidden folder
  leaves this page entirely, along with the bundle. The key carries the node id, `pub.folder_dir`
  resolves it against the same listing the layout was built from, and the map below never sees it.
%>
<%#
  EVERY PANEL IN A SLOT WITH ITS WIDTH (2026-09-09, the page editor; spec §3): the same
  `div.panel-slot.panel--<width>` the maker's page renders, from the same layout, so the visitor
  sees the arrangement the maker made. The body is CAPTURED first because every partial below
  omits itself when it has nothing to show, and an empty slot would still hold a grid cell.

  ONE SLOT PER PANEL AND NONE AROUND A REPO CARD: a card is not a panel of the layout — it has no
  key, no width and no place in the order — so it stays a direct child of the grid, which
  pages/projects.css already sizes (`.project-panels > .panel, .project-panels > .panel-slot`).

  THE GRID AROUND THESE SLOTS IS projects/_panel_groups (2026-09-09, tabs), the same shape the
  maker's page renders: one grid when the layout has no tab, and the top group, the bar, the panes
  and the repository cards' own grid when it has (it held Publish too until that became the edit
  area's Publishing tab, 2026-10-26 — the visitor's page never rendered Publish either way).
%>
<% cards = capture do %>
  <% pub.repo_cards.each_value do |card| %>
    <%= render "public/projects/repo_card", card: card %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
<%= render layout: "projects/panel_groups",
           locals: { groups: pub.layout.groups(pub.visible_panels), layout: pub.layout, tail: cards.presence } do |key| %>
  <% body = capture do %>
    <% if key == "wiring" %>
      <% if pub.connections.any? %>
        <%= render "projects/wiring", project: project, connections: pub.connections, editable: false, layout: pub.layout %>
      <% end %>
    <% elsif pub.layout.block?(key) %>
      <%= render "public/projects/block_panel", pub: pub, key: key, block: pub.blocks_by_key[key] %>
    <% elsif pub.layout.folder?(key) %>
      <%= render "public/projects/folder_panel", pub: pub, key: key, dir: pub.folder_dir(key),
                 view: pub.layout.view_for(key) %>
    <% elsif (partial = Maker::PublicProject::PANEL_PARTIALS[key]) %>
      <%= render partial, pub: pub %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
  <% if body.present? %>
    <div class="panel-slot panel--<%= pub.layout.width_for(key) %>"><%= body %></div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
